The '''X-League''' ({{nihongo|Xリーグ|Ekkusu Rīgu}}), officially the '''X-League Professional Lurandian Football''' ({{nihongo|Xリーグプロ鎧球|Ekkusu Rīgu Puro Gaikyū}}), is the highest level of professional [[Lurandian Football]] in [[Okaiken]]. Founded in 1985, the league currently comprises twenty corporate-backed teams organized into two divisions. The X-League is the second most-watched professional sports league in Okaiken, trailing only [[Okaiken Rugby League One]].

The league's season runs from September through January, with the regular season determining playoff seeding and the postseason culminating in the [[Rice Bowl]], the national championship game. The X-League is known for its intense corporate-backed rivalries, deeply loyal fan bases, and a style of play that emphasizes aggressive passing, punishing special teams, and high-impact collisions consistent with the {{nihongo|[[Cult of Iron]]|鉄身信仰|Tesshin-shinkō}} cultural movement.

The [[Toyozawa Ironblades]] are the league's most successful franchise, having won nine Rice Bowl titles. The defining franchise rivalry of the modern era is between the Ironblades and the [[Okami Direwolves]], whose annual matchups routinely draw the highest regular season viewership in Okaiken across all sports.

[[File:X-League game action.jpg|thumb|A Division 1 game at Okami National Stadium during the 2024 season]]

==History==
===Founding and early years (1985–1992)===
The X-League was formally established on March 15, 1985, by the [[Sanese Lurandian Football Association]] (SLFA) as a consolidation of the existing corporate amateur Lurandian Football circuit. The founding six teams were all backed by major Sanese industrial conglomerates, reflecting the corporate sports model that also defined Okaiken's rugby and basketball leagues.

The inaugural 1985 season featured six corporate founding franchises: the [[Toyozawa Electric|Toyozawa Ironblades]], the [[Okami Financial Group|Okami Direwolves]], the [[Saitama Heavy Industries|Soma Forges]] (later rebranded as the Soma Samurai), the [[Tokuyama Steel Corporation|Tokuyama Steelworkers]] (subsequently renamed the Tokuyama Steelers), the [[Aizawa Shipbuilding|Aizawa Iron Hawks]], and the [[Shinagawa Marine Industries|Shinagawa Mariners]] (reorganized in 2004 as the Shinagawa Kraken).

The first Rice Bowl under the X-League banner was held on January 12, 1986, with the Toyozawa Ironblades defeating the Soma Forges 24–17 in front of 32,000 fans at Toyozawa Municipal Stadium. The Ironblades, led by star fullback [[Shigeo Arakawa]], established early dominance, winning four of the first six Rice Bowl titles.

[[File:X-League inaugural 1985.jpg|thumb|The inaugural 1985 X-League season]]

===First expansion (1993–2000)===
The league expanded to eight teams in 1993 with the addition of the [[Minatojima Tide]] and the [[Sendai Blitz]], reflecting the sport's geographic spread beyond the Okami–Toyozawa corridor. The expansion coincided with the first television broadcasting agreement, which brought X-League games to a national audience via the OR Sports network.

The late 1990s were defined by the rise of the [[Okami Direwolves]], who won three consecutive Rice Bowl titles from 1997 to 1999 behind the precision passing of quarterback [[Tatsuo Kanzaki]] and a dominant defensive front. The Direwolves' three-peat established the franchise as the Ironblades' primary rival and set the stage for the most enduring franchise rivalry in Sanese professional sports.

===Second expansion (2001–2010)===
The league expanded to twelve teams in 2001, adding the [[Mito Blacksmiths]], the [[Yonezawa Vipers]], the [[Gunma Thunder]], and the [[Akita Frost Wolves]]. The 2000s saw increasingly competitive balance, with five different franchises winning the Rice Bowl between 2000 and 2010. The Tokuyama Steelers won their first Rice Bowl in 2003, and the Shinagawa Kraken—rebranded from the original Mariners in 2004—claimed their first title in 2007.

===Modernization and Forge State era (2011–present)===
The [[Jutsu Restoration]] of 2011 and the ascendancy of the {{nihongo|Forge State|鍛冶国家|Kaji Kokka}} ideology brought unprecedented attention and investment to the X-League. The sport's emphasis on armored physical confrontation aligned naturally with the national philosophy, and government investment through the [[National Iron Body Initiative]] designated Lurandian Football as a priority sport for youth development.

The league underwent an extensive modernization campaign beginning in 2014, establishing a tiered two-division structure featuring automatic promotion and relegation between Division 1 and Division 2. This competitive overhaul was complemented in 2016 by the formal implementation of a standardized corporate salary cap and rookie draft mechanism designed to maintain parity across regional teams. Expanded nationwide broadcast contracts signed with DAZN Okaiken and Tesshin TV brought every regular-season and postseason contest to national streaming and cable audiences, driving a 340 percent increase in annual league revenue.

The 2014 restructuring coincided with the peak of the {{nihongo|Iron Curtain|鉄のカーテン|Tetsu no Kāten}}—the dominant defensive unit of the [[Okami Direwolves]], anchored by linebacker [[Naoto Kongo]] and defensive end [[Kengo Shibata|Kengo "The Wall" Shibata]]. The Kongo–Shibata pairing, which operated from 2012 to 2017, was the most feared defensive combination in X-League history, combining Kongo's sideline-to-sideline pursuit with Shibata's devastating pass rush. The Iron Curtain propelled the Direwolves to the 2015 Rice Bowl title—Kongo's MVP performance in that game is widely regarded as one of the greatest individual defensive performances in league history.

Further expansion in 2019 added the [[Yamagata Anvils]], [[Okazaki Phoenix]], [[Sanda Hammers]], and [[Fukushima Blaze]], bringing the league to sixteen teams. A final expansion in 2023 added the [[Haruno Storm]], [[Ehime Anchors]], [[Ishijima Ironworks]], and [[Iwate Crushers]], establishing the current twenty-team structure and cementing the X-League's nationwide footprint.

The modern X-League era has been defined by the Ironblades–Direwolves franchise rivalry, personified by Ironblades quarterback [[Ryuichi Nakada]]'s annual clashes with the Direwolves' defense. During the 2016–2019 seasons, the Nakada–Kongo matchup was the league's marquee individual rivalry; after Kongo's retirement in 2019, the rivalry continued at the franchise level. The 2024 regular season meeting drew a record 52.3 million viewers—the highest-rated regular season broadcast in Okaiken television history for any sport.

